  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nVentricular tachycardia with ICD firing\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nThis is a ___ year old male with coronary artery disease s/p MI \nand CABG in ___, systolic congestive heart failure, \nhypertension, hyperlipidemia, ventricular tachycardia s/p AICD \nplacement and ablation x 2 now presents with his AICD firing and \nsustained ventricular tachycardia at an outside hospital.  \nPatient states he was at restaurant, went to the bathroom and \nhis AICD implant fired once just after urinating, then again \nafter he sat back down to dinner.  Patient reports being totally \nasymptomatic, with no complaints of palpitations, chest pain, \ndyspnea, or lightheadedness.  Patient was concerned and went to \n___ Emergency Department.  There, he was found to be in \nventricular tachycardia at rate 150s-170s, got metoprolol but \nthe ventricular tachycardia did not resolve.   He was \ncardioverted back to normal sinus rhythm (200 J).  Patient was \nbriefly hypotensive (not known how low), but recovered.  He was \nloaded with amiodarone and then maintained on a drip at 1 \nmg/min. During his last admission at ___, patient was \ndischarged on quinidine and Toprol XL but quinidine was \nsubsequently discontinued secondary to side effects \n(thrombocytopenia).  Patient was transferred to ___ for \nfurther management.\n.\nIn the ED, initial vital signs were T 98 HR 79 BP 102/75 RR 16 \nO2 sat 98% on 2L NC.  Cardiology was consulted.  Apparently, \nAICD will detect rate >170 but will only fire if rate >188.  EKG \nhere showed NSR, TWI some old and some new.  Labs showed a \ntroponin of 0.07, INR 2.7 (goal ___.  He was admitted to \ncardiology.  At the time of transfer, vital signs were HR 80 RR \n15 BP 105/68 O2 sat 98% on 2L NC. \n.  \nOn admission to the floor, patient reported no problems.  There \nwas no chest pain, dyspnea, abdominal pain, palpitations or \ndizziness.  He had not returned back to ventricular tachycardia \nsince arriving to ___.\n.  \nOn review of systems, he denied any prior history of stroke, \nTIA, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, bleeding at the \ntime of surgery, myalgias, joint pains, cough, hemoptysis, black \nstools or red stools.  He denied recent fevers, chills or \nrigors.  All of the other review of systems were negative.  \n.  \nCardiac review of systems were notable for absence of chest \npain, dyspnea on exertion, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, \northopnea, palpitations, syncope or presyncope.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nThis is a ___ year old male with coronary artery disease s/p MI \nand CABG in ___, congestive heart failure, hypertension, \nhyperlipidemia, ventricular tachycardia s/p AICD placement and \nablation x 2 now presents with his AICD firing at home and \nsustained ventricular tachycardia at an outside hospital.\n.\nACTIVE ISSUES\n-------------\n# Ventricular tachycardia: patient presented to ___ in normal \nsinus rhythm.  He was previously on mexilitene and sotalol, and \nswitched to quinidine and metoprolol on his last admission.  His \nquinidine was discontinued due to agranulocytosis.  He presented \nto ___ on an amiodarone IV drip, which was continued for 24 \nhours and then switched to PO amiodarone.  He was continued on \nhis home dose of metoprolol.  Patient did not go back into \nventricular tachycardia during his time admitted. Patient is \nbeing discharged on a regimen of amiodarone 400 mg PO BID for \none week after discharge, and then 400 mg PO daily until told \notherwise.  He is discharged on his home dose of metoprolol.  \nPatient will also require pulmonary function tests in the near \nfuture due to starting amiodarone.  Liver and thyroid function \ntests were checked during admission, and are included in this \nreport.  He will follow up with Dr. ___ as an outpatient, \nas well as his PCP.  He will also schedule an appointment with \nhis cardiologist in ___.\n.\nINACTIVE ISSUES\n---------------\n# Coronary artery disease: s/p CABG in ___.  Patient reported \nno chest pain, dyspnea, nausea, vomiting or diaphoresis during \nhis episode of ventricular tachycardia.  He was continued on his \nhome dose of lisinopril, metoprolol, aspirin and simvastatin.\n.\n# Systolic congestive heart failure: ejection fraction is \nsupposedly 30%, though there is no echocardiogram at ___ \ndocumenting this.  Patient was continued on his home dose of \nmetoprolol and lisinopril during his admission.  He did not \nrequire diuresis while admitted.\n.\n# Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation: patient is on coumadin at \nhome.  He is on metoprolol for rate control.  Patient was in \nnormal sinus rhythm when presenting to ___ and was rate \ncontrolled.  He was continued on his home dose of warfarin \nduring his admission, and INR was trended daily.  His warfarin \nwas cut down to 5 mg daily after starting amiodarone.  He will \nhave his INR check at his PCP's office on the day after \ndischarge.  His PCP ___ follow up all results.\n.\n# Hypertension: patient was normotensive on presentation to \n___.  He was continued on his home dose of lisinopril and \nmetoprolol, which he will be discharged on.\n.\n# Hyperlipidemia: patient was continued on his home dose of \nsimvastatin during admission.  His simvastatin dosage was cut \ndown due to him now being on amiodarone.  His lipid panel should \nbe rechecked after discharge.\n.\n# Obstructive sleep apnea: patient is on a CPAP at home, which \nwas ordered for him during his hospital stay.  He should \ncontinue this upon discharge.\n.\nTRANSITION OF CARE\n------------------\n# Code status: patient is full code, confirmed  \n.\n# Follow-up: patient will follow up with his PCP and Dr. \n___ electrophysiologist, for which appointments have \nbeen scheduled.  He will schedule an appointment with his \ngeneral cardiologist on his own.  His INR needs to be followed \nup as an outpatient, which will be checked the day after \ndischarge.  Patient will also require pulmonary function tests \nin the near future due to starting amiodarone.  Liver and \nthyroid function tests were checked during admission, and are \nincluded in this report.  He will need his lipid levels \nrechecked at some point by his PCP due to the decrease in his \nsimvastatin dose.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary diagnosis:\n\nVentricular tachycardia\n\nSecondary diagnosis:\n\nCoronary artery disease\nSystolic congestive heart failure\nParoxysmal atrial fibrillation\nHypertension\nHyperlipidemia",
